"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[137],{58273(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"barriers","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/barriers","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":7,"items":[{"id":"failed-proofs/cryptography-vs-natural-proofs","title":"Cryptography vs. Natural Proofs","description":"Secure encryption needs functions that look random but aren\'t. If such functions exist, they hide their own simplicity so well that the natural way of proving hardness can\'t see it. The thing we want to prove protects itself.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/cryptography-vs-natural-proofs"},{"id":"failed-proofs/limits-of-relativization","title":"Limits of Relativization","description":"If oracle-blind proofs can\'t work, a proof has to look inside the machine. In 1990, a technique called arithmetization did exactly that, proved a famous result that cannot relativize, and briefly made people think the first barrier had fallen.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/limits-of-relativization"},{"id":"failed-proofs/summary-of-the-barriers","title":"Summary of the Barriers","description":"Phase 4 in one place: every barrier, what it blocks, what slips past it, and the 2011 result that showed a way around all of them for a weaker question. Why a proof may need mathematics that doesn\'t exist yet.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/summary-of-the-barriers"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-algebrization-barrier","title":"The Algebrization Barrier","description":"Arithmetization broke through the first barrier. In 2008, Aaronson and Wigderson upgraded relativization to cover it, and showed that the upgraded version still can\'t settle P vs NP. A second wall, right behind the first.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-algebrization-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-natural-proofs-barrier","title":"The Natural Proofs Barrier","description":"Razborov and Rudich noticed that every circuit lower bound proof of their time had the same simple shape. Then they proved that, if strong cryptography exists, no proof of that shape can ever separate P from NP.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-natural-proofs-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier","title":"The Relativization Barrier","description":"In 1975, Baker, Gill and Solovay built two imaginary worlds: one where P equals NP, and one where it doesn\'t. Any proof that works the same in both worlds can\'t settle the question. That rules out the most natural attack of all.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/why-havent-we-solved-it","title":"Why Haven\'t We Solved It?","description":"Over fifty years, thousands of researchers, and almost no progress on the main question. It\'s not lack of effort. It\'s that mathematicians have proved, three separate times, that whole families of proof techniques cannot work.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/why-havent-we-solved-it"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);